{
  "description": "issue #688 (terminal slice): item 2 of the normative same-tick delivery list, `pending on_key events in registration order` (spec/interaction-events.md:84-89). Two handlers are registered for the SAME key `x` and ONE key press is delivered at tick 1, so both become pending at the same drain point and their relative order can come only from the handler list -- not from the host schedule, which carries a single entry here. They fire in registration order: the line-1 handler prints 1 before the line-2 handler prints 2. The order is pinned TWICE over, independently: by the printed values, and by the handler-start `instruction` events (spec/interaction-events.md:101-102, `the start of a handler block emits an instruction event for the block-head that caused the handler to run`), whose spans are the line-1 `on_key` head (seq 5) before the line-2 head (seq 8) -- so a run that reversed delivery fails on both assertions. The sibling `on-key-registered-twice` proves only that a duplicate registration REGISTERS both handlers and explicitly records that neither fires; recognition is not delivery, so before this fixture the corpus pinned item 2 of that list at no shared drain point at all (the cross-kind fixture `dispatch/cross-kind-order-during-wait` has exactly one handler per kind, which cannot discriminate intra-kind order). `wait` still emits its `primitive` after the pause completes (seq 11), and no event payload carries the tick, the key, or a `turtle_id` -- a key press concerns no turtle.",
